# LeakSanitizer suppressions for PathFinder (used by ASan's bundled LSan).
#
# Usage:
#   ASAN_OPTIONS="verify_asan_link_order=0:detect_leaks=0" \
#   LSAN_OPTIONS="suppressions=$(pwd)/sanitizers/asan.supp" \
#       make launch-car HEADLESS=true
#
# IMPORTANT: the entries below use LeakSanitizer's `leak:` syntax, which means
# this file must be passed via LSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=..., NOT
# ASAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=.... Passing a leak-only file via ASAN_OPTIONS makes
# ASan exit with "failed to parse suppressions" before your program runs.
#
# The two env-var settings ABOVE in tandem do two different things:
#  - ASAN_OPTIONS=verify_asan_link_order=0   silences the foxglove_bridge dlopen
#                                            startup warning (not a leak; can't
#                                            be put in a suppression file)
#  - ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0             skips leak detection entirely; the
#                                            LSan suppressions below are only
#                                            consulted if you flip this to 1
#  - LSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=this-file     filters known-benign leaks (below)
#
# foxglove_bridge startup warning explained:
#
#   ==NNN==ASan runtime does not come first in initial library list; you should
#   either link runtime to your application or manually preload it with LD_PRELOAD
#
# That fires once when foxglove_bridge dlopen()s its connector plugins from a
# non-ASan-instrumented .so. It's benign: those plugins don't allocate in ways
# ASan cares about, and our ASan-instrumented code is still fully checked. Set
# verify_asan_link_order=0 to silence it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leak suppressions (active only when ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1).
#
# We default to detect_leaks=0 because Gazebo and rclcpp shutdown paths leak
# small amounts on graceful exit; chasing them is not worth the time and they
# don't grow over the lifetime of a run. If you do enable leak checking, the
# patterns below filter the known-benign culprits so real leaks in our code
# stand out.

# Gazebo's gz-sim8 GUI singleton + Qt plugin caches leak on shutdown.
leak:libgz-sim8

# FastDDS holds internal singleton state for the lifetime of the process; the
# leak shows up as a "definitely lost" block at exit.
leak:libfastrtps
leak:libfastcdr

# rclcpp's logging subsystem registers handlers via static initialization that
# isn't torn down on rclcpp::shutdown().
leak:rclcpp::Logger
leak:rcutils_logging
